4. If Bruno does not have proxy settings

You can try launching Bruno with proxy environment variables.

Close Bruno completely and run the following in PowerShell:

$env:HTTP_PROXY="http://127.0.0.1:8089"
$env:HTTPS_PROXY="http://127.0.0.1:8089"

Then launch Bruno from that same PowerShell session using its executable path.

For example, if installed in the default location:

& "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\Programs\bruno\Bruno.exe"

The exact executable location may differ. Also, whether Bruno honors these variables depends on its version and HTTP client implementation.

5. If you get an SSL error in Bruno

After fixing the proxy, you may encounter a certificate error.

If that happens, check whether Bruno trusts your corporate CA certificate. Importing the approved corporate CA is preferable to disabling certificate validation.

For an authorized SIT testing environment, you can temporarily disable SSL certificate verification in Bruno to isolate the issue, then re-enable it.

